{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind Adventure in Japan**\n\nLast month, I escaped to Japan for ten days—an unforgettable blur of neon lights, ancient temples, and tiny ramen bowls. My journey began in **Tokyo**, where skyscrapers stood shoulder-to-shoulder with shrines like **Meiji-jingū**. I got lost in **Shibuya’s** scramble crossing, ate my first **tamagoyaki** (sweet omelet) at a standing lunch bar, and marveled at the **tsukiji Outer Market** before sunrise. A short train ride to **Nikko** led me to towering cedars and hidden waterfalls, where deer grazed near crumbling samurai-era gates.\n\nFrom there, the **Shinkansen (bullet train)** whisked me to **Kyoto**, where I wandered through **Fushimi Inari’s** endless torii gates, sipped matcha in a 300-year-old teahouse, and watched geisha glide past at **Gion**. A day trip to **Nara** introduced me to friendly, bowing deer and the colossal **Tōdai-ji** Buddha. Back in Kyoto, I squeezed into an **onsen** (hot spring) with strangers, scrubbing my skin raw in the ritualistic baths.\n\nMy final stop was **Osaka**, where I devoured **takoyaki** from a street cart and rode the **Universal Studios Japan** roller coaster—twice. The trip ended with a last-minute detour to **Kobe**, where I dined on melt-in-your-mouth beef and watched fireworks over the harbor.\n\nJapan defied my expectations: chaotic yet orderly, futuristic yet steeped in tradition. Every street corner held a story—whether it was the grand, the mundane, or something in between. Already planning my return.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.27843376994132996}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.037113383412361145}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.018043426796793938}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -4.7801782784517854e-05}, {"token": " Adventure", "logprob": -0.8805687427520752}, {"token": " in", "logpr